{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, before he had finished speaking.</b> One should reflect on the addition of the word הוא, for Scripture should properly have said 'and it came to pass when he finished speaking,' but the word הוא is the name of the Holy One, blessed be He — as it is written (Psalms 100:3) 'הוא עשנו ולא אנחנו,' and (Numbers 18:23) 'ועבד הלוי הוא.' It is hinted in the expression הוא that there was with him the assistance of Elohim in that mission, by means of the angel over whom Abraham prayed — 'He will send His angel before you' (Genesis 24:7). And Scripture hints to you that the angel preceded him, so that before he could finish his words the angel was already there at the spring of water to prepare Rebecca for him — this is what it says: 'והנה רבקה יוצאת,' for the word והנה is an expression of preparation. And immediately 'וירץ העבד לקראתה,' because he saw that the waters rose toward her, as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted. Then the servant reflected upon what Abraham had told him — that the angel of HaShem was with him. And so he recounted to the household what Abraham had said to him: 'He will send His angel with you and make your journey prosper'; for this reason you will find that from that moment, once it was verified to him that the angel was with him, he is called in the parashah 'the man' (איש). For until now he was called by the name 'servant': 'וישם העבד,' 'ויקח העבד,' 'וירץ העבד.' From here onward he is called 'man,' after the name of the angel who was with him, who is called 'man,' as it is written (Daniel 9:21) 'והאיש גבריאל,' and (Genesis 24:21 — in our chapter) 'ויאמר האיש נסעו מזה,' according to the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory. Therefore he is called after that angel who went with him. And he began to say 'והאיש משתאה' — that is, lingering and waiting on her account, for he was intent and reflecting upon the matter of the angel promised to him, through observing the success of the events. From this the Scripture mentions him by the name 'the man': 'ויקח האיש,' 'ויקוד האיש,' 'כה דבר אלי האיש,' 'ויבא האיש הביתה.' And when Laban and Bethuel permitted him to take Rebecca and said, 'הנה רבקה לפניך קח ולך' — which is the completion of the mission and the sealing of the journey's success — the servant reverted to his former state, and Scripture mentioned him with the expression 'servant' as at first. This is what it says: 'ויוצא העבד כלי כסף וכלי זהב,' 'ויקח העבד את רבקה וילך,' 'ותאמר אל העבד,' 'ויאמר העבד,' 'ויספר העבד ליצחק.' And one should not raise a difficulty from 'התלכי עם האיש הזה,' for these are not the words of the Torah but the words of her brother and her mother, and it would not be fitting for them to say 'התלכי עם העבד הזה,' for that is not the way of proper conduct and would not be respectful to her. And what is written, 'ותלכנה אחרי האיש' — these being words of the Torah — this was for Rebecca's honor, for one could not say 'ותלכנה אחרי העבד,' because the Torah was concerned for the honor of Rebecca and her maidens. Or one may say further that 'האיש משתאה לה' hints at the angel, who lingered and waited by the spring of water on her account, and from that point onward the servant was called by the name 'the man.' And what strengthens this matter for you is what it says at the end of the parashah: 'ותלכנה אחרי האיש ויקח העבד את רבקה וילך' — this explains that 'the man' and 'the servant' are two distinct matters, one different from the other."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:51.719064918Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}